{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/failed-hypothesis-reset.md", "content": "# Resetting after failed ARC hypotheses

Repeated incorrect outputs indicate the task class or answer representation may be wrong, not merely a coordinate detail. Before spending another attempt, revisit all demonstrations: list input/output dimensions, identify separators and partitions, enumerate each region's candidate features, and test a compact region-summary mapping across every pair. Do not submit a revised pattern unless it exactly reproduces all known demonstrations. A new demo request should target a clearly stated unresolved distinction; serial requests without analysis impose cost without improving inference.

For small outputs from large partitioned canvases, prioritize understanding the relationship between each cell/region and the compact answer. Keep a candidate mapping table and explicitly account for exceptions (e.g. output cells retaining a background/category color). Only after the mapping reproduces all demos should it be applied to the test.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u019/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/failed-hypothesis-reset.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-recovery", "file": "references/task-217fc4ad-review.md", "content": "# Session review: premature speculative submissions after multiple demos

This instance supplied six substantially different input/output examples, generally with a full-width or full-height divider and outputs that retained one side/panel in reduced form. No shared deterministic encoding was established by the demos. The test was a 9×2 grid containing 5s and a 7, but repeated guesses alternated among one-column, two-column recolorings, and all-zero outputs; an identical one-column guess was even submitted twice. Those candidates were not supported by exact replay against the demonstrations and all failed.

Reusable recovery lesson: after every rejection, record the exact candidate grid and its hypothesis before acting. Immediately verify the next proposed action is materially different and has evidence; if it is not, stop. Never let attempt-budget pressure or an unresolved task justify arbitrary candidates. On a demo turn, first identify what the new pair adds to the common rule; only request again for a stated discriminator between live hypotheses. This case remained unresolved, so it provides a process warning, not a transformation rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u019/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-evidence-recovery/references/task-217fc4ad-review.md"}
